---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import DeptCalendar from '../components/DeptCalendar.astro';

// 공식 2026 달력 기준: 12일 주기, 2026-01-01 = C (사이클 인덱스 0)
const LACO_CYCLE = ['C', 'A', 'B', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'C', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'B'];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is LA County Fire on a 48/96?',
    a: 'No — a common misconception repeated by scheduling blogs. The county\'s own published work schedule shows a 12-day cycle in which each platoon works four separated 24-hour shifts (on, off, on, off, off, on, off, on) and then gets four consecutive days off. It averages the same 56 hours a week as 48/96, but you never work two tours back to back.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the platoon colors?',
    a: 'On the official calendar A platoon is black, B is red, C is green — the classic fire-service three-color scheme. Pick your letter above; the math is the same regardless of color.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the "56-hour corridor" on the official calendar?',
    a: 'Payroll period boundaries. LACoFD averages 56 duty hours per week, and the yellow corridor bars mark where pay-period accounting starts and stops. They affect paychecks, not which days you work — your duty days are fully determined by the 12-day cycle shown here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this an official LACoFD publication?',
    a: 'No — it is an independent tool aligned to the official 2026 Work Schedule PDF published by the department, and verified against January and February 2026. For payroll-grade questions use the official document; for "am I on duty on Christmas," this page answers instantly.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="LA County Fire Shift Calendar — A/B/C Platoons, 12 Months"
  description="Interactive LACoFD shift calendar aligned to the official 2026 work schedule. Pick your platoon (A, B, C) and see every 24-hour duty day for 12 months, holidays answered, exportable to your phone."
  h1="LA County Fire Shift Calendar"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    The Los Angeles County Fire Department runs one of the more distinctive rotations in American
    fire service — a <strong>12-day cycle</strong> of four separated 24-hour tours followed by a
    four-day break. Pick your platoon and see your entire year, aligned to the department's
    <a href="https://fire.lacounty.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2026-Shift-Calendar.pdf" rel="noopener">official 2026 Work Schedule</a>.
  </p>

  <DeptCalendar
    cycle={LACO_CYCLE}
    anchor="2026-01-01"
    platoons={['A', 'B', 'C']}
    shiftLabel="24-hr Duty Day"
    icsName="lacofd-shift"
  />

  <h2>Reading the 12-day cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Within each 12-day round, your platoon works day 1, is off day 2, works day 3, gets two days
    off, works day 6, is off day 7, works day 8 — and then the reward: <strong>four consecutive
    days off</strong> before the cycle restarts. Three platoons interlock so exactly one is on duty
    every day of the year. The structure is philosophically the opposite of
    <a href="/48-96-shift-calendar">48/96</a>: instead of concentrating work into one 48-hour tour,
    LACoFD separates every tour with at least one recovery day and still delivers a four-day break
    each cycle. Sleep scientists tend to approve; commuters count the extra round trips.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twelve does not divide into seven, so the cycle drifts through the week — your four-day break
    sweeps across weekends on a slow, fully predictable schedule that the 12-month view above makes
    obvious. That predictability is the point of this page: which platoon has Christmas, whose
    break swallows Thanksgiving weekend, and where your breaks meet a school holiday are all facts
    you can know today.
  </p>

  <h2>Notes for planning</h2>
  <p>
    A duty day on the calendar is the day your 24-hour tour begins — the tour on the 20th runs into
    the morning of the 21st, which matters when you are reading the holiday list (a Christmas Eve
    tour eats Christmas morning). Trades move single tours, so the holiday answers above are also
    your trade map: generate a teammate's platoon in a second tab and both sides of a swap are on
    screen. For other departments, see the
    <a href="/firefighter-shift-calendar">firefighter calendar hub</a> — including
    <a href="/houston-fire-department-shift-calendar">Houston's four-shift system</a> and the
    <a href="/cal-fire-shift-calendar">CAL FIRE 3-on-4-off model</a>.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
